ution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-3,1)$ and $B(1,-2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(1,-2)-f(-3,1) \\\\  & =(1)^{2}(-2)-(-3)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =-2-9 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046fdfb7-5908-5b1c-90e7-05dd1219d510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256819} {:answer/id #uuid "0494c2e3-4f93-51c9-97b4-abdfd2bbd422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256820} {:answer/id #uuid "0c87d320-18b0-5f77-bb7b-34ad58ca38b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256902} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc2089f-6561-53d8-b5ee-acdc74d9f43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257182} {:answer/id #uuid "c02c18c1-a46b-54b2-8cf2-af2aecbb3d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c87d320-18b0-5f77-bb7b-34ad58ca38b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256902} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f5a55be-ee74-53ab-9b52-5cbae09ff6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d312ac8-d0a5-5f87-aa22-804248c3f5d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125732" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-\\pi ,0)$ to $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-\\pi ,0)$ and $B(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(-\\pi ,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =(1-1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15deefdc-05ae-5086-a5f0-c1dc6044e9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256977} {:answer/id #uuid "2247900f-b21b-584e-a22b-427516ec3d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257062} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2d5949-bfc8-5763-bd36-6a74f995a834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257566} {:answer/id #uuid "7187435a-9b2a-5138-bdc3-8e258afbb442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257779} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8bcb5c-084b-52f8-b5f5-1853ba91623b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7187435a-9b2a-5138-bdc3-8e258afbb442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257779} :answer-field/id #uuid "568ddf7f-5eb1-53a7-8971-663ff621802f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30e624bb-f7e4-561a-be9c-3d2d751b4f13" :question/math-academy-id "q-125735" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,1)$ to $(0,3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,1)$ and $B(0,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,3)-f(-2,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{0}^{2}+1}-\\frac{1}{(-2)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06828943-2517-5788-afbf-83f2f355cc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256836} {:answer/id #uuid "a816432d-4895-55c2-953a-1699f10ab4b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258249} {:answer/id #uuid "c83059f8-61f6-50d7-9ebe-a77e51cd568a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258528} {:answer/id #uuid "f86cf986-18fe-5093-aa80-ac15fc51982d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258960} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7fca20-5bdf-5366-9789-fd9cfe5d4ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f86cf986-18fe-5093-aa80-ac15fc51982d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258960} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e700bf1-abf6-54f9-a024-9c52cdfe7394" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0148bc8-aabc-5dbc-868a-3905d42c3ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-125751" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}-2x)\\,i+2xy\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,1)$ to $(1,3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(1(3)^{2}-{1}^{2})-(0(1)^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
